From 0ef47071cb4fe7a4da19390e2a0dd67607aecdb1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Turnstone <turnstone@agents.invalid>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 17:28:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Turnstone: the version seam for the five api symbols, and a
 correction to my own drift number

Two things, and the second one is the more important.

THE SEAM. New api/utils/VersionCompat holds the whole of the api half of 26.1..26.3
support: vanillaPack(), worldDataLayer(), worldDataRegistries(), chat(Gui) and
toasts(Minecraft). Each asks for what the thing IS rather than what it is called -
its type, its return type, its position in a layering, which registries it contains
- because a released Fabric jar is remapped to intermediary names, and remapping
rewrites the references in the byte code and not the strings in the constants, so a
lookup by name that works on a dev box would not work in the jar. Every lookup
asserts it found exactly one candidate rather than taking the first: a silent wrong
answer here is a crash inside a remapped jar with no line number to point at. The
world's registries are found by the fact that the set carries the world presets and
not the level stem - checked in both jars' static initialisers, where the client's
synchronised set is a strict subset (28 of 45, and 32 of 51) and the dimension set is
the one with the level stem. Four layers, order verified in both jars, not assumed.

Verified in a live client on 26.3, because these paths cannot be unit-tested: the
vanilla registries will not initialise in a plain JUnit JVM at all (I tried, and the
failure was environmental - RegistryLayer.<clinit> at line 18 - so the test was
removed rather than left failing or faked). Instead: Baritone's own chat lines render
through chat(); `#mine minecraft:stone` parses a block and prints it through the
registry path; and the toast path puts "[Baritone] true" on the screen through
toasts(). No new exception in the log. compileApiJava -Pminecraft_version=26.1 is
green.

THE CORRECTION. My published "six symbols in three files, all peripheral glue" was a
measurement artefact and I have retracted it in the plan. compileJava does NOT compile
the whole tree: the root build makes main depend on the api project's output, so the
task stopped at the six api errors and never compiled src/main/java at all. With
`compileJava compileLaunchJava --continue` the real figure is 71 error sites, 20
distinct causes, 13 files - and 52 of the 71 are one subsystem, the renderer, where
26.1 has no StagedVertexBuffer, no BindGroupLayouts and no renderpearl RenderPipeline
at all. That is a port, not a rename. The rest is a genuine mix: swing() gained an
argument (5 sites), ServerboundPunchPacket was ServerboundSwingPacket (4), screen()
and openUri() and setScreen() changed shape, and a few constants and one entity class
moved.

What that changes: the api half of multi-version support is DONE, the main half is
not, V2 is L rather than S/M, and anyone picking it up should start from
docs/planning/2026-10-11/evidence/turnstone-261-main-compile.log rather than from my
earlier table. The multiversion-build design stays withdrawn, but for a better reason
than I first gave: not because the drift is small - it is not - but because 52 of 71
sites sit in one subsystem, which a per-version abstraction handles and a second build
system does not. What is unchanged and still true: the pathfinder, path execution, the
movement rules, the process framework and the humanlike layer are not in the 71.

26.3 suite green throughout: 407 tests, 0 failures.
